At Apple's current prices, the &zwnj;iPhone 18 Pro&zwnj; is clearly the stronger choice for most buyers. It costs just &#36;100 more than the Air at every shared storage capacity, yet adds excellent Ultra Wide and Telephoto cameras, a variable-aperture main camera, longer battery life, stereo speakers, faster charging, vapor chamber cooling, a newer chip, and more.<br/>
<br/>
The &zwnj;iPhone Air&zwnj; makes a stronger case in the hand than it does on a specification sheet. It is 46 grams lighter than the &zwnj;iPhone 18 Pro&zwnj;, has a slightly larger display, and feels unlike any other iPhone. With its polished titanium frame and frosted glass back, it looks more classy and premium than the &zwnj;iPhone 18 Pro&zwnj; in many respects. If appearance, thinness, weight shape how much you enjoy using a phone every day, choosing the Air can be worthwhile despite its camera, speaker, and battery limitations. The Pro Max offers a 6.9-inch screen and even longer battery life, but weighs 84 grams more than the Air.<br/>
<br/>
Retail pricing can change that calculation. The Air has seen substantial discounts, including an <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/03/30/iphone-air-discounts-continue/">unlocked 256GB model for &#36;899.99</a> earlier this year. If you find a new &zwnj;iPhone Air&zwnj; for around &#36;900 while the &zwnj;iPhone 18 Pro&zwnj; remains &#36;1,199, the roughly &#36;300 saving makes the Air much more compelling if you can live with one rear camera and shorter battery life.<br/>
<br/>
